From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 02:58:29 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Retire the four audits whose subjects have been exercised

AthenaIndexAudit was always meant to be deleted once the structures it guards had
run in anger. Four of them have, with no divergence ever reported:

  EntityPlayer.queuedChunks vs chunkCoordIntPairQueue      patch 0161
  pendingTickListEntriesThisTickSet vs the list it mirrors patch 0161
  Block.hasRedstonePowerHandler() vs the skipped call      patch 0161
  the chunk the light queries reuse vs the provider's      patch 0164

They are 38 to 41 patches old and a single nine minute session puts tens of
thousands of checks through the first three and 83 million through the light one.
The hooks came out of World and WorldServer entirely, so the audit now reaches
into two files instead of four.

Two stay, because their subjects are days old rather than months:

  ChunkProviderServer.creatureCensus, and the two sites that adjust it. The site
  check found a real divergence in live play last week - a decrement applied to a
  class entityCount had no entry for - so this one has already paid for itself.

  Chunk's X bucket index and the per-slice IMonster and EntityHuman counts.

ChunkCoordQueueIndex.derivedFrom, matches and describeDifference are left in
place. They are the executable statement of that index's invariant and
ChunkCoordQueueIndexTest still exercises them; only the per-tick caller is gone.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

